# Build-context exclusions.
#
# The Dockerfile still names every directory it copies explicitly, and that is
# the guard that matters — an exclusion list fails OPEN, so anything added later
# is included unless somebody remembers to add it here. This file is about
# BUILD SPEED: without it the whole context, node_modules included, is uploaded
# to the daemon on every build.
node_modules
**/node_modules
.git
.env
.env.*
tests
eval
sim
scripts
# ...except the migration runner, which the Cloud Run migration job needs. The
# Dockerfile names it explicitly; without this negation that COPY fails.
!scripts/migrate.js
# ...and the staging seeder, run through the same job to put one synthetic
# business in a database that has schema and no rows. It refuses to run
# unless the database AND instance names both contain "staging".
!scripts/seed-staging.js
# ...and the read-only inspector, for questions only the real database can
# answer — the instances are private-IP only, so there is no psql from a laptop.
!scripts/db-inspect.js
# ...and Lane C's two verification entrypoints, which run through the same job
# for the same reason. BOTH of these had to be added in TWO places — a COPY in
# the Dockerfile and a negation here — and the Dockerfile alone is not enough:
# with the COPY present and the negation missing, the build fails with
# `"/scripts/c7-restore-parity.js": not found`, which reads as a missing file
# in the repository rather than an excluded build context. Found by building
# the image rather than by reading about it.
!scripts/c8-rls-proof.js
!scripts/c7-restore-parity.js
!scripts/import-tenant.js
docs
latency-runs
infra
AI-phone-dashboard
.superpowers
*.log
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
